## Chapter 1 Introduction

The theory of Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) describes interactions between particles never directly seen in nature, quarks and gluons, referred to collectively as partons. Partons, in addition to leptons, are well established as the building blocks of matter. Three generations of quarks have been observed, up (\(u\)) and down (\(d\)), strange (\(s\)) and charm (\(c\)), and bottom (\(b\)) and top (\(t\)). These and their corresponding anti-quarks (\(\overline{u},\overline{d},\overline{s},\overline{c},\overline{b},\overline{t}\)) constitute all known hadronic matter. Table 1 lists known characteristics of the quarks [49].

Quarks are never seen as individual particles. Instead, they are strongly bound within hadrons which are further classified into baryons (\(qqq\)) and mesons (\(q\overline{q}\)). The most common examples of baryons are protons (\(uud\)) and

