## Chapter 6 Results and Discussion

### 6.1 Experimental Results

The data presented here are for collisions with a primary vertex \(z\)-position cut of \(|z|<75\) cm and reconstruction cuts of Table 5.1. Also, data is binned in centrality according to Table 4.1.

#### 6.1.1 \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) to \(\Xi^{-}\) Ratio

The dependence of the \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) yields on such variables as transverse momentum (mass), rapidity, and centrality requires knowledge of efficiency corrections. However, as the STAR detector response is assumed to be symmetric with respect to \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) reconstruction, the raw yields are sufficient information to produce \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}/\Xi^{-}\) and to examine said ratio as a function of these variables. Effects due to absorption of anti-particles, especially anti-protons, in the detector material and feed-down \(\Omega^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Omega}^{+}\) can potentially affect the \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}/\Xi^{-}\) ratio. The absorption of \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\), \(\overline{\Lambda}\), and \(\overline{p}\) as a function of \(p_{\perp}\) has been examined and affects the integrated ratio by only 0.2%. Figure 6.1 shows the absorption rate for \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) particles up to 2 GeV/c in transverse momentum given an input distribution flat in \(p_{\perp}\). By \(p_{\perp}=1\) GeV/c, the absorption rate has fallen to near zero. The overall effect is to increase the number of \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) by onlya handful of counts and so is neglected in this analysis. The feed-down contribution comes from the \(\Omega^{-}\to\Xi^{-}\pi^{0}\) channel which has a 8.6% branching ratio [49] and has also been neglected here.

