Seen as a function of rapidity in Figure 6.3, the \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}/\Xi^{-}\) ratio again does not deviate significantly from a constant. The flatness of \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}/\Xi^{-}\) seems to indicate that the production mechanisms for \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) are similar in the rapidity range examined. This is also an indication that the incident baryons are mostly removed from the mid-rapidity region. The BRAHMS collaboration verified this by measuring the \(\overline{p}/p\) ratio as a function of rapidity. They found a ratio of \(0.66\pm 0.04\pm 0.06\) at mid-rapidity and a ratio of \(0.41\pm 0.04\pm 0.06\) at 2 units of rapidity [32]. This supports the argument that there is a significant degree of collision transparency, even if the mid-rapidity region is not completely net-baryon free. For \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\overline{\Xi}^{+}\) though, this dependence is even weaker since the majority of the valence quarks are from \(s\overline{s}\) production rather than from the incident baryons.

